---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe how you implemented argument parsing.  How do
>> you arrange for the elements of argv[] to be in the right order?
>> How do you avoid overflowing the stack page?

process_execute() copies the command line into a fresh page and uses
the first word (truncated to 15 characters) as the thread name, so the
"name: exit(n)" message has no arguments in it.

load() makes another copy and uses strtok_r(" ") to get the first word,
which is the executable to open.

setup_stack() maps one zeroed page just below PHYS_BASE, then:

  1. Splits a copy of the command line with strtok_r(" ") into a
     tokens[] array (max 128 arguments).  strtok_r skips repeated
     spaces, so "a   b" gives two arguments.
  2. Copies each string onto the stack, last argument first, and
     overwrites tokens[i] with the string's new user address.
  3. Rounds esp down to a multiple of 4.
  4. Pushes a NULL pointer (argv[argc]).
  5. Pushes tokens[argc-1] ... tokens[0].  Because we push from the
     last element to the first and the stack grows down, argv[0] ends
     up at the lowest address, which is the correct array order.
  6. Pushes argv (the address of argv[0]), then argc, then a fake
     return address of 0.

Overflow: before writing anything, setup_stack() adds up the string
lengths plus 3 bytes of alignment plus (argc + 4) pointers (argv[]
entries, the NULL sentinel, argv, argc, return address).  If this is
more than PGSIZE, it returns false and the load fails, so exec()
returns -1 instead of writing below the stack page.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A3: Why does Pintos implement strtok_r() but not strtok()?

strtok() keeps its position in a hidden static variable.  In a kernel
several threads can be parsing at the same time, and a thread can be
preempted in the middle of parsing, so a shared static would be
corrupted.  strtok_r() keeps the position in a caller-supplied
save_ptr, which lives on each thread's own stack, so it is reentrant
and thread-safe.

>> A4: In Pintos, the kernel separates commands into a executable name
>> and arguments.  In Unix-like systems, the shell does this
>> separation.  Identify at least two advantages of the Unix approach.

1. Less code runs in the kernel.  A parsing bug in a user-level shell
   only crashes the shell; a bug in kernel parsing can crash or
   compromise the whole system.

2. Flexibility.  The shell can do quoting, wildcards, variables,
   pipes and redirection, and different shells can parse differently,
   all without changing the kernel.  The kernel just receives a ready
   argv[] array.

