>> B2: Describe how file descriptors are associated with open files.
>> Are file descriptors unique within the entire OS or just within a
>> single process?

Each process has a list of struct file_desc.  open() calls
filesys_open(), mallocs a file_desc, gives it fd = next_fd++ and
appends it to the list.  read/write/seek/tell/filesize/close look the
number up in the current thread's list with find_fd().  0 and 1 are
never handed out; they are handled specially as keyboard and console.

Descriptors are unique only within a single process.  Two processes
can both have an fd 2 that refers to different files.  They are not
inherited by children.  Opening the same file twice gives two
different fds, each with its own struct file and its own position.

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

We use the "check first, then dereference" method:

  - valid_range(ptr, size) checks every byte in [ptr, ptr+size):
    the pointer must be non-NULL, below PHYS_BASE (is_user_vaddr) and
    mapped in the current page directory (pagedir_get_page != NULL).

  - argument(f, i) reads the i-th 32-bit word above the user esp.
    The syscall number is argument 0.  It calls valid_range on the
    four bytes first, and kills the process if they are bad.

  - copy_string(user, kernel, size) copies a user string one byte at
    a time into a kernel page, checking each byte, until it sees the
    '\0'.  File names and exec command lines are always copied into
    kernel memory before use.

  - For read and write, the whole buffer is checked with valid_range
    before any data moves.  Then file_read/file_write/putbuf use the
    user pointer directly; this is safe because every byte has just
    been checked and the page cannot be unmapped by another process.

If any check fails, terminate_bad_user() sets exit_status = -1 and
calls thread_exit().

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full page (4,096 bytes) of data
>> to be copied from user space into the kernel.  What is the least
>> and the greatest possible number of inspections of the page table
>> (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that might result?  What about
>> for a system call that only copies 2 bytes of data?  Is there room
>> for improvement in these numbers, and how much?

Our valid_range() calls pagedir_get_page() once per byte, so a
4,096-byte buffer always costs 4,096 inspections (fewer only if it
stops early at a bad byte), and a 2-byte buffer costs 2.

The theoretical minimum is the number of distinct pages the buffer
touches: a full page needs 1 inspection if it is page-aligned and 2
if it is not; 2 bytes need 1, or 2 if they straddle a page boundary.

So there is a lot of room for improvement: checking once per page
instead of once per byte would bring 4,096 down to at most 2.  An even
better option is to only check ptr < PHYS_BASE and let the MMU detect
unmapped pages through page_fault() (get_user/put_user), which needs
no page-table inspections at all in the common case.  We kept the
per-byte version because it is the simplest to read and is obviously
correct.

>> B5: Briefly describe your implementation of the "wait" system call
>> and how it interacts with process termination.

wait(pid) calls process_wait(pid):

  1. Search the current thread's children list for a child_info with
     that tid.  If none is found (not my child) or waited is already
     true, return -1 straight away.
  2. Set waited = true and sema_down(&wait_sema).
  3. When woken, read exit_status, remove the record from the list,
     and call release_child(record, parent).

On termination, process_exit() in the child stores its exit_status
into its child_record, does sema_up(&wait_sema), and calls
release_child(record, child).  If the child has already exited before
wait is called, wait_sema already has value 1, so sema_down returns
immediately and the saved status is returned.  A process killed by
the kernel keeps exit_status = -1 (the default), so wait returns -1.

>> B6: Any access to user program memory at a user-specified address
>> can fail due to a bad pointer value.  Such accesses must cause the
>> process to be terminated.  System calls are fraught with such
>> accesses, e.g. a "write" system call requires reading the system
>> call number from the user stack, then each of the call's three
>> arguments, then an arbitrary amount of user memory, and any of
>> these can fail at any point.  This poses a design and
>> error-handling problem: how do you best avoid obscuring the primary
>> function of code in a morass of error-handling?  Furthermore, when
>> an error is detected, how do you ensure that all temporarily
>> allocated resources (locks, buffers, etc.) are freed?  In a few
>> paragraphs, describe the strategy or strategies you adopted for
>> managing these issues.  Give an example.

Strategy 1: put the checks in small helpers.  argument(),
valid_range() and copy_string() do all the checking and kill the
process themselves if something is wrong.  The syscall code just
says "fd = argument (f, 1)" and never has to test for failure,
because a bad pointer never returns.

Strategy 2: validate everything before taking any resources.  Each
syscall reads its arguments and checks its buffers first, and only
then acquires filesys_lock.  So we never kill a process while it is
holding the lock.  Temporary kernel pages (for file names) are freed
on the failure path right before terminate_bad_user().

Strategy 3: everything a process owns is freed in one place,
process_exit(), which runs no matter how the process dies (exit(),
bad pointer, or exception).  It closes all fds, re-allows writes to
the executable, releases child records and destroys the page
directory.

Example - write(fd, buffer, size):
  - argument(f,1..3) each check 4 bytes of the user stack; bad esp ->
    exit(-1).
  - valid_range(buffer, size) checks the whole buffer; bad -> exit(-1).
    No lock is held yet, nothing to free.
  - Only then: fd 1 -> putbuf(); otherwise find_fd(), take
    filesys_lock, file_write(), release the lock.
  - If the process is later killed, process_exit() closes its files.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?

process_execute() creates the child_info record (load_sema = 0) and
passes it to the new thread, then calls sema_down(&load_sema) and
blocks.  In start_process() the child calls load(), stores the
result in child->load_success, and then does sema_up(&load_sema).
The parent wakes up and reads load_success: if false it drops the
record and returns TID_ERROR (-1), otherwise it returns the tid.

The record is on the heap and is in the parent's children list
before the child starts, so it is valid even if the child exits
immediately after signalling.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?

The shared child_info has two owners, P and C.  release_child()
clears the caller's "alive" flag and frees the record if both flags
are now false.  It does the flag update and the "both gone?" test
with interrupts disabled, so P and C cannot both free it, and cannot
both skip freeing it.

  - P waits before C exits: P blocks on wait_sema.  C writes
    exit_status, sema_up, release_child (C).  P wakes, reads the
    status, release_child (P) -> record freed.

  - P waits after C exits: C has already written the status, upped
    wait_sema and dropped its ownership.  P's sema_down returns
    immediately, it reads the status and frees the record.

  - P exits without waiting, before C exits: P's process_exit()
    calls release_child (P) on every record in its children list.
    C keeps running; when it exits it calls release_child (C) and
    frees the record.

  - P exits without waiting, after C exits: C already dropped its
    ownership, so P's release_child frees the record.

The struct thread of C is always freed by the scheduler when C dies,
independently of the record.  Special cases: waiting twice or on a
non-child returns -1 because of the waited flag and the per-process
children list; if load fails, C still exits normally and P drops its
ownership in process_execute(), so the record is freed.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?

Checking first with is_user_vaddr() and pagedir_get_page() is the
simpler of the two documented methods.  It needs no changes to
page_fault() and no inline assembly, and it is easy to reason about:
a pointer is never dereferenced until it has been checked.  The cost
is speed (see B4), which does not matter for this project.

>> B10: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?

Advantages: a linked list per process is simple, has no fixed limit
on open files, uses memory only for files that are actually open,
and makes per-process fds and cleanup at exit straightforward.

Disadvantages: lookup is O(n) in the number of open files, and fds
are never reused (next_fd only grows), so a process that opens and
closes files many times gets ever larger numbers.  An array indexed
by fd would give O(1) lookup and number reuse.

>> B11: The default tid_t to pid_t mapping is the identity mapping.
>> If you changed it, what advantages are there to your approach?

We did not change it; pid == tid.  Each user process is exactly one
thread, so a separate pid type would add code without any benefit.
A different mapping would only help if a process could have several
threads.

